{
  "an9.20:0.1": "Numbered Discourses 9.20 ",
  "an9.20:0.2": "2. The Lion’s Roar ",
  "an9.20:0.3": "About Velāma ",
  "an9.20:1.1": "At one time the Buddha was staying near Sāvatthī in Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery. ",
  "an9.20:1.2": "Then the householder Anāthapiṇḍika went up to the Buddha, bowed, and sat down to one side. The Buddha said to him, ",
  "an9.20:2.1": "“Householder, I wonder whether your family gives gifts?” ",
  "an9.20:2.2": "“It does, sir. ",
  "an9.20:2.3": "But only coarse sour gruel with false black pepper.” ",
  "an9.20:2.4": "“Householder, someone might give a gift that’s either coarse or fine. ",
  "an9.20:2.5": "But they give it carelessly, thoughtlessly, not with their own hand. They give the dregs, and they give without consideration for consequences. ",
  "an9.20:2.6": "Then wherever the result of any such gift manifests, their mind doesn’t incline toward enjoyment of nice food, clothes, vehicles, or the five refined kinds of sensual stimulation. ",
  "an9.20:2.7": "And their children, wives, bondservants, servants, and workers don’t want to listen to them. They don’t actively listen or try to understand. ",
  "an9.20:2.8": "Why is that? ",
  "an9.20:2.9": "Because that is the result of deeds done carelessly. ",
  "an9.20:3.1": "Someone might give a gift that’s either coarse or fine. ",
  "an9.20:3.2": "And they give it carefully, thoughtfully, with their own hand. They don’t give the dregs, and they give with consideration for c